    "collected_at": "2026-10-22T14:49:19.310523+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/this-pedal-powered-rv-is-small-and-light-yet-tough-enough-to-do-burning-man-277388.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/this-pedal-powered-rv-is-small-and-light-yet-tough-enough-to-do-burning-man-277388.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-10-22T11:51:59+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-22T11:51:59+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-22T14:59:16.376118+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-22T14:59:16.376118+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nThis Pedal-Powered RV Is Small and Light, Yet Tough Enough to Do Burning Man\n22 photos\nPhoto: YouTube/Attoparsec\nThe modern society is driven by consumerism, which, in turn, is driven mostly by artificially created needs, and that's exactly the opposite of what Burning Man stands for. So what better way to make a return to Black Rock City than with a DIY project that's half functional product and half art installation?\nThat's an accurate description for the Bike RV, a pedal-powered DIY shelter on wheels built almost from scratch by Matthew \"Fish\" Dockery, best known as Attoparsec on YouTube. Dockery is a Seattle-based fabricator and artist who takes pride in his \"radically impractical\" builds, whether they are machinery, gadgets, or kinetic sculptures.\nIn 2026, he decided to return to Burning Man after a hiatus of 10 full years, and to do so with a build that would serve as shelter and means of transportation around the City in the Black Rock Desert in Nevada. He called that build the Bike RV, though perhaps a better description would be a pedal-powered shelter of the kind that are so popular at the community and art festival.\nHe documented the build and the following adventure at Burning Man in a video he posted on his channel, which you will find in full at the bottom of the page. It's not the kind of tutorial that will give you real-life advice or information you can use in your own build , but it's an interesting inside look at the culture of Burning Man.\nPhoto: YouTube/Attoparsec It's an alternative solution to millions of DIY builds that populate the online world, built with functionality in mind and very little concern to comfort. The Bike RV is a basic shelter in the manner of the hexayurt that inspired it, so it's low-cost, rapidly deployable, and surprisingly tough.\nMore importantly, it's all these things in a tutorial where storytelling takes center stage. To paraphrase a highly rated comment, it's a video where you come for the Bike RV and stay for Dockery's storytelling skills.\nThe build started with a heavy-duty cargo trike of an unknown make and brand, with a bent rear wheel and a single brake line that ironically rendered braking impossible. Dockery calls it the \"most unpleasant riding experience I've ever had\" after his first test ride, whose \"very existence was morally offensive,\" and he sets out to fix the issues to have a solid base for the weight he'd end up adding.\nPhoto: YouTube/Attoparsec He also added a differential built with a ready-made kit to allow turning, since that had provenvirtually impossible on the test ride, and gave the trike a full paintjob to cover the existing mint green one.\nUsing tubular aluminum, he built a frame extension that would hold the future construction , which he imagined as a sort of capsule room, but on wheels. He'd later realize his mistake in not moving the axle farther to the rear of the trike, since the added weight posed a serious danger of tipping the whole thing over when he was inside.\nHe compensated for that with a pair of trailer jacks, which added stability to the rear-heavy rig. With the base of the RV on risers, he was left with lots of cargo space between frame and base, so he decided to use that to add a 12V Euhomy fridge on sliders, running off a 200W Jackery solar system he'd bought the summer before.\nPhoto: YouTube/Attoparsec The structure was built with 1-inch (2.5-cm) polyisocyanurate foam insulation panels held together with fiberglass tape, with the edges covered in aluminum so the foam interior wouldn't crumble away. Dockery speaks of interior lights and a modular interior, but he doesn't show any footage from inside, with the sole exception of his view of the raw panels lying down.\nThe construction was based on the H8 Danger Ranger hexayurt design, measuring 8 feet (2.4 meters) in length and 4 feet (1.2 meters) in width. He opted to transport it with the roof removed in the back of a U-Haul truck and have it assembled on site.\nInvented in 2002 by Vinay Gupta, a hexayurt is an open-source temporary shelter in various configurations, imagined as a fast, cheap, and environmentally-friendly housing solution for disaster relief. Hexayurts are a common sight at Burning Man, so the Bike RV fit right in.\nPhoto: YouTube/Attoparsec So, with this rushed construction, Dockery headed out to Burning Man, where he set up camp after securing the roof of the Bike RV with long straps. He found that moving across the Playa was smooth, though not necessarily fast or effortless, and was able to enjoy his first day even though he had to stop frequently to hammer in the wheel that kept sliding off the axle.\nThough most attendees now used e-bikes to move across the City, Dockery was satisfied with his solution, as heavy and faulty as it was. It proved even more useful on the second night, when a powerful storm and strong rains set in.\nHe eventually had to move into the back of the U-Haul truck when the Bike RV started showing signs of water ingress, and eventually packed up and left when the road dried. Still, he appreciated how the structure handled a full night of battering winds and scattered rain, despite the fact that it was not up to his construction (and artistic) standards.\nPhoto: YouTube/Attoparsec Dockery's conclusion is that Burning Man is no longer his scene, but he doesn't regret this final piece of functional art he created to attend this year.\nVia // Attoparsec on YouTube\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nDIY bike RV bicycle viral story Burning man alternative housing US\nAbout the author: Elena Gorgan\nElena has been writing for a living since 2006 and, as a journalist, she has put her double major in English and Spanish to good use. She covers automotive and mobility topics like cars and bicycles, and she always knows the shows worth watching on Netflix and friends. Full profile\nUP NEXT CarPlay Is Getting the Google Maps Feature Many Android Auto Users Hate",
